{"text": "Curtis Jason Goodrich (\n\nRedacted\n\nApril 29, 2014\nMark A. Hall, Senior Associate\nSpelman & Johnson Group\n\nDear Mr. Hall:\n\nI would appreciate the opportunity to meet with you as a candidate for Chief of Police at\nThe University of Cincinnati. My resume reflects my commitment to training and\neducation, knowledge of law enforcement practices, experience in campus settings, anda\nteam building leadership style. I have over 11 years of executive experience in an 18 year\ncareer, and I would like to share my vision with you for further developing the culture of\nsuccess at The University of Cincinnati into a national model.\n\nI possess extensive experience at all levels of law enforcement, and ] am a graduate of the\nNorthwestern University Center for Public Safety's School of Police Staff and Command as\nwell as an FBI regional command college. I have earned a Master's Degree in Conflict\nManagement as well as a Bachelor\u2019s Degree in Criminal Justice. My ability to communicate\nboth orally and in writing is one of my strongest assets. I began my sworn career in 1995\nand have accrued knowledge of key campus topics such as the Clery Act, Title IX issues,\nemergency management concepts, and the impact of technology on campus security. [ have (\ntaught leadership courses to all levels of law enforcement and presented nationally on\nmanaging bias, conflict management, building law enforcement collaborations, and Title Ix\ninvestigations.\n\nMy career progression through four states has provided me with a thorough understanding\nof federal and state legal requirements. I believe fully in the team concept and have a i\nsuccessful track record of cooperative relationship building. I consider honesty and !\nintegrity to be the most important qualifications of police officers, and I choose to lead by\nexample. I have a demonstrated commitment to diversity issues, and stand as the only\npolice officer and male awarded the Mary Jane Werthan Award for the advancement of\nwomen\u2019s issues from the Vanderbilt University Women\u2019s Center.\n\nI believe my background makes me uniquely qualified to lead policing efforts at The\nUniversity of Cincinnati. My proficiency within the higher education environment\ndemonstrates I possess the skills needed to excel in this position. I believe my proven\ncommitment to community policing would benefit the campus.\n\nIn closing, it would be an honor to lead the University of Cincinnati in expanding its role of\nservice. I look forward to speaking with you soon. I can be reached 1 Ex\ni Thank you for your\n\nconsideration.",
